WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.601
I was only four years old

00:00:17.625 --> 00:00:20.536
when I saw my mother
load a washing machine

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.742
for the very first time in her life.

00:00:23.203 --> 00:00:25.179
That was a great day for my mother.

00:00:25.691 --> 00:00:28.536
My mother and father
had been saving money for years

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.600
to be able to buy that machine,

00:00:30.624 --> 00:00:32.670
and the first day it was going to be used,

00:00:32.694 --> 00:00:35.833
even Grandma was invited
to see the machine.

00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:36.993
(Laughter)

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:39.536
And Grandma was even more excited.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.860
Throughout her life, she had been
heating water with firewood,

00:00:43.884 --> 00:00:47.894
and she had hand-washed laundry
for seven children.

00:00:47.918 --> 00:00:52.946
And now, she was going to watch
electricity do that work.

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.811
My mother carefully opened the door,

00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:01.264
and she loaded the laundry
into the machine,

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:03.487
like this.

00:01:03.511 --> 00:01:05.712
And then, when she closed the door,

00:01:05.736 --> 00:01:07.536
Grandma said, "No, no, no, no!

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.560
Let me! Let me push the button!"

00:01:10.584 --> 00:01:11.596
(Laughter)

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.242
And Grandma pushed the button,

00:01:14.266 --> 00:01:16.821
and she said, "Oh, fantastic!

00:01:16.845 --> 00:01:20.607
I want to see this! Give me a chair!
Give me a chair! I want to see it,"

00:01:20.631 --> 00:01:23.362
and she sat down in front of the machine,

00:01:23.386 --> 00:01:26.977
and she watched
the entire washing program.

00:01:27.001 --> 00:01:28.003
(Laughter)

00:01:28.027 --> 00:01:29.452
She was mesmerized.

00:01:29.878 --> 00:01:34.306
To my grandmother,
the washing machine was a miracle.

00:01:35.226 --> 00:01:38.658
Today, in Sweden and other rich countries,

00:01:38.682 --> 00:01:42.418
people are using
so many different machines.

00:01:42.442 --> 00:01:44.427
Look -- the homes are full of machines.

00:01:44.451 --> 00:01:46.536
I can't even name them all.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.043
And they also, when they want to travel,

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.231
they use flying machines

00:01:52.255 --> 00:01:54.923
that can take them to remote destinations.

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.172
And yet, in the world,

00:01:56.196 --> 00:01:59.980
there are so many people
who still heat the water on fire,

00:02:00.004 --> 00:02:02.495
and they cook their food on fire.

00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:04.581
Sometimes they don't even
have enough food.

00:02:05.231 --> 00:02:07.536
And they live below the poverty line.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.421
There are two billion fellow human beings

00:02:10.445 --> 00:02:13.152
who live on less than two dollars a day.

00:02:13.176 --> 00:02:16.311
And the richest people over there --
there's one billion people,

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:19.673
and they live above
what I call the "air line" --

00:02:19.697 --> 00:02:20.714
(Laughter)

00:02:20.738 --> 00:02:24.183
because they spend
more than 80 dollars a day

00:02:24.207 --> 00:02:26.100
on their consumption.

00:02:26.124 --> 00:02:28.773
But this is just one, two,
three billion people,

00:02:28.797 --> 00:02:31.588
and obviously, there are
seven billion people in the world,

00:02:31.612 --> 00:02:35.085
so there must be one, two,
three, four billion people more

00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:38.287
who live in between
the poverty and the air line.

00:02:38.311 --> 00:02:40.056
They have electricity,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.830
but the question is:
How many have washing machines?

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.381
I've done the scrutiny of market data,
and I've found that, indeed,

00:02:48.405 --> 00:02:51.977
the washing machine
has penetrated below the air line,

00:02:52.001 --> 00:02:55.254
and today, there's an additional
one billion people out there

00:02:55.278 --> 00:02:57.536
who live above the "wash line."

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.536
(Laughter)

00:02:59.969 --> 00:03:03.605
And they consume
for more than 40 dollars per day.

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:06.367
So two billion have access
to washing machines.

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:09.318
And the remaining five billion --

00:03:09.342 --> 00:03:10.608
how do they wash?

00:03:10.632 --> 00:03:12.238
Or, to be more precise,

00:03:12.262 --> 00:03:15.609
how do most of the women
in the world wash?

00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:18.844
Because it remains the hard work
for women to wash.

00:03:19.589 --> 00:03:21.650
They wash like this: by hand.

00:03:22.698 --> 00:03:26.995
It's hard, time-consuming labor,

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:30.196
which they have to do
for hours every week.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.369
And sometimes they also have
to bring water from far away

00:03:33.393 --> 00:03:34.822
to do the laundry at home,

00:03:34.846 --> 00:03:38.408
or they have to bring the laundry
away to a stream far off.

00:03:38.432 --> 00:03:40.608
And they want the washing machine.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:44.840
They don't want to spend
such a large part of their life

00:03:44.864 --> 00:03:46.227
doing this hard work

00:03:46.251 --> 00:03:48.709
with so relatively low productivity.

00:03:49.352 --> 00:03:51.564
And there's nothing
different in their wish

00:03:51.588 --> 00:03:53.279
than it was for my grandma.

00:03:53.303 --> 00:03:55.648
Look here, two generations
ago in Sweden --

00:03:55.672 --> 00:03:57.536
picking water from the stream,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.405
heating with firewood
and washing like that.

00:04:00.429 --> 00:04:03.411
They want the washing machine
in exactly the same way.

00:04:03.435 --> 00:04:06.755
But when I lecture
to environmentally concerned students,

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:10.882
they tell me, "No, everybody in the world
cannot have cars and washing machines."

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.159
How can we tell this woman

00:04:13.183 --> 00:04:15.749
that she isn't going
to have a washing machine?

00:04:15.773 --> 00:04:19.185
And then I ask my students --
over the last two years, I've asked --

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:21.233
"How many of you don't use a car?"

00:04:21.257 --> 00:04:25.098
And some of them proudly raise their hand
and say, "I don't use a car."

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.891
And then I put the really tough question:

00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:31.828
"How many of you hand-wash
your jeans and your bedsheets?"

00:04:31.852 --> 00:04:34.536
And no one raised their hand.

00:04:35.198 --> 00:04:39.536
Even the hardcore in the green movement
use washing machines.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:41.701
(Laughter)

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:45.707
So how come [this is]
something that everyone uses

00:04:45.731 --> 00:04:47.618
and they think others will not stop it?

00:04:47.642 --> 00:04:48.891
What is special with this?

00:04:48.915 --> 00:04:51.779
I had to do an analysis
about the energy use in the world.

00:04:51.803 --> 00:04:52.969
Here we are.

00:04:52.993 --> 00:04:55.509
Look here. You see
the seven billion people up there?

00:04:55.533 --> 00:04:57.536
The air people, the wash people,

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.348
the bulb people and the fire people.

00:05:00.372 --> 00:05:02.389
One unit like this

00:05:02.413 --> 00:05:07.536
is an energy unit of fossil fuel --
oil, coal or gas.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:11.147
That's what most of the electricity
and the energy in the world is.

00:05:11.171 --> 00:05:14.290
And it's 12 units used
in the entire world,

00:05:14.314 --> 00:05:17.121
and the richest one billion,
they use six of them.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.913
Half of the energy is used
by one seventh of the world population.

00:05:20.937 --> 00:05:23.076
And these ones, who have washing machines

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.067
but not a house full of other machines,

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:26.281
they use two.

00:05:26.907 --> 00:05:29.163
This group uses three, one each.

00:05:29.789 --> 00:05:31.361
And they also have electricity.

00:05:31.385 --> 00:05:33.536
And over there,
they don't even use one each.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.881
That makes 12 of them.

00:05:35.329 --> 00:05:37.000
But the main concern

00:05:37.024 --> 00:05:40.695
for the environmentally interested
students -- and they are right --

00:05:40.719 --> 00:05:42.169
is about the future.

00:05:43.154 --> 00:05:44.320
What are the trends?

00:05:44.344 --> 00:05:48.003
If we just prolong the trends,
without any real advanced analysis,

00:05:48.027 --> 00:05:49.194
to 2050,

00:05:49.218 --> 00:05:52.346
there are two things
that can increase the energy use:

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:55.355
first, population growth;
second, economic growth.

00:05:55.957 --> 00:05:59.423
Population growth will mainly occur
among the poorest people here,

00:05:59.447 --> 00:06:01.285
because they have high child mortality

00:06:01.309 --> 00:06:03.136
and they have many children per woman.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.043
And that will get you two extra,

00:06:05.067 --> 00:06:07.299
but that won't change
the energy use very much.

00:06:07.323 --> 00:06:09.323
What will happen is economic growth.

00:06:09.347 --> 00:06:12.103
The best of here
in the emerging economies --

00:06:12.127 --> 00:06:13.826
I call them "the New East" --

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.536
they will jump the air line.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.199
"Wopp!" they will say.

00:06:17.223 --> 00:06:20.658
And they will start to use as much
as the Old West are doing already.

00:06:20.682 --> 00:06:21.758
(Laughter)

00:06:21.782 --> 00:06:24.063
And these people,
they want the washing machine.

00:06:24.087 --> 00:06:25.536
I told you. They'll go there.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.390
And they will double their energy use.

00:06:27.414 --> 00:06:30.538
And we hope that the poor people
will get into the electric light.

00:06:30.562 --> 00:06:33.974
And they'll get a two-child family
without a stop in population growth.

00:06:33.998 --> 00:06:37.217
But the total energy consumption
will increase to 22 units.

00:06:37.241 --> 00:06:40.043
And these 22 units --

00:06:40.067 --> 00:06:42.652
still, the richest people
use most of them.

00:06:44.122 --> 00:06:45.497
So what needs to be done?

00:06:45.521 --> 00:06:47.498
Because the risk,

00:06:47.522 --> 00:06:50.265
the high probability
of climate change is real.

00:06:50.677 --> 00:06:52.081
It's real.

00:06:52.105 --> 00:06:54.872
Of course, they must be
more energy efficient.

00:06:55.776 --> 00:06:57.890
They must change
their behavior in some way.

00:06:57.914 --> 00:07:00.350
They must also start
to produce green energy,

00:07:00.374 --> 00:07:01.599
much more green energy.

00:07:01.623 --> 00:07:04.995
But until they have the same
energy consumption per person,

00:07:05.019 --> 00:07:06.930
they shouldn't give advice to others --

00:07:06.954 --> 00:07:08.450
what to do and what not to do.

00:07:08.474 --> 00:07:09.598
(Laughter)

00:07:09.622 --> 00:07:10.800
(Applause)

00:07:10.824 --> 00:07:15.569
Here, we can get more
green energy all over.

00:07:15.593 --> 00:07:17.241
This is what we hope might happen.

00:07:17.265 --> 00:07:19.248
It's a real challenge in the future.

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:23.295
But I can assure you
that this woman in the favela in Rio,

00:07:23.319 --> 00:07:25.088
she wants a washing machine.

00:07:25.112 --> 00:07:27.474
She's very happy
about her minister of energy

00:07:27.498 --> 00:07:29.474
that provided electricity to everyone --

00:07:29.498 --> 00:07:33.075
so happy that she even voted for her.

00:07:33.099 --> 00:07:34.845
And she became Dilma Rousseff,

00:07:34.869 --> 00:07:39.049
the president-elect of one of the biggest
democracies in the world,

00:07:39.073 --> 00:07:42.281
moving from minister
of energy to president.

00:07:42.305 --> 00:07:46.186
If you have democracy,
people will vote for washing machines.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:47.695
They love them!

00:07:47.719 --> 00:07:48.985
(Laughter)

00:07:49.496 --> 00:07:51.280
And what's the magic with them?

00:07:51.304 --> 00:07:54.536
My mother explained
the magic with this machine

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.037
the very, very first day.

00:07:57.061 --> 00:07:58.571
She said, "Now, Hans.

00:07:59.535 --> 00:08:01.020
We have loaded the laundry.

00:08:01.044 --> 00:08:02.761
The machine will make the work.

00:08:02.785 --> 00:08:04.926
And now we can go to the library."

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:08.360
Because this is the magic:
you load the laundry,

00:08:08.384 --> 00:08:10.666
and what do you get out of the machine?

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.991
You get books out of the machines,

00:08:14.015 --> 00:08:15.167
children's books.

00:08:15.191 --> 00:08:17.536
And mother got time to read for me.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.725
She loved this.

00:08:18.749 --> 00:08:22.536
I got the "ABC's" -- this is why
I started my career as a professor,

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:24.700
when my mother had time to read for me.

00:08:24.724 --> 00:08:26.536
And she also got books for herself.

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.315
She managed to study English
and learn that as a foreign language.

00:08:30.339 --> 00:08:32.646
And she read so many novels,

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:35.110
so many different novels here.

00:08:35.134 --> 00:08:38.800
And we really,
we really loved this machine.

00:08:38.824 --> 00:08:39.962
(Laughter)

00:08:39.986 --> 00:08:42.536
And what we said, my mother and me,

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:45.032
"Thank you, industrialization.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.494
Thank you, steel mill.

00:08:47.518 --> 00:08:49.312
Thank you, power station.

00:08:49.336 --> 00:08:52.360
And thank you,
chemical processing industry

00:08:52.384 --> 00:08:54.392
that gave us time to read books."

00:08:54.416 --> 00:08:55.633
Thank you very much.

00:08:55.657 --> 00:08:56.739
(Laughter)

00:08:56.763 --> 00:09:03.763
(Applause)

